<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teresa of Ávila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1515">1515</a>–<a href="/years/1582">1582</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Saint Teresa of Ávila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teresa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> was a Spanish Carmelite nun and mystic who reformed her order, founding the Discalced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross">John of the Cross</a> from 1562. Her writings, including her Life and The Interior Castle, are classics of Spanish prose and Christian mysticism. She was canonised in 1622 and in 1970 became the first woman named a Doctor of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila.jpg" alt="Teresa of Ávila" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo Balaca · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santa_Teresa_de_Jes%C3%BAs_(Museo_del_Prado).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
